@module flutter-m04 | Material Design
@summary Build polished Material 3 interfaces with themes, components, forms and accessibility.

@lesson flutter-m04-l01 | Material 3 fundamentals | 8
## Learning objectives
- Understand Material 3 principles
- Enable Material 3 in ThemeData

## Material 3 fundamentals
Material 3 (Material You) is Google's latest design system: tonal colour palettes generated from a seed, larger rounded shapes, new components such as NavigationBar and FilledButton, and dynamic colour. Since Flutter 3.16 it is on by default, so new apps get M3 styling automatically. Design with *roles* (primary, surface, onSurface) rather than raw colours so light and dark themes both work.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

final theme = ThemeData(
  useMaterial3: true,
  colorScheme: ColorScheme.fromSeed(seedColor: const Color(0xFF6750F5)),
);
```

## Summary
- Material 3 is the default design system in modern Flutter.
- Use colour roles from the ColorScheme instead of hard-coded colours.

@lesson flutter-m04-l02 | ColorScheme | 8
## Learning objectives
- Generate and use a ColorScheme
- Pick the right role for each element

## ColorScheme
A `ColorScheme` holds about 30 colour roles. `primary` is for key actions, `secondary`/`tertiary` for accents, `surface` variants for backgrounds and cards, `error` for problems, and every role has an `on...` counterpart for content placed on it, guaranteeing contrast.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget chip(BuildContext context) {
  final c = Theme.of(context).colorScheme;
  return Container(
    color: c.primaryContainer,
    padding: const EdgeInsets.all(8),
    child: Text('Pro', style: TextStyle(color: c.onPrimaryContainer)),
  );
}
```

## Summary
- ColorScheme.fromSeed builds an accessible palette.
- Pair each background role with its on-role for text and icons.

@lesson flutter-m04-l03 | ThemeData | 8
## Learning objectives
- Centralise styling in ThemeData
- Customise component themes

## ThemeData
`ThemeData` configures colours, typography and default styles for every Material component. Customise component themes (`appBarTheme`, `cardTheme`, `filledButtonTheme`, `inputDecorationTheme`) once instead of styling each widget.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

final theme = ThemeData(
  colorScheme: ColorScheme.fromSeed(seedColor: Colors.teal),
  cardTheme: const CardTheme(margin: EdgeInsets.all(8)),
  inputDecorationTheme: const InputDecorationTheme(border: OutlineInputBorder()),
);
```

## Summary
- Style once in ThemeData; widgets inherit it.
- Read it with Theme.of(context).

@lesson flutter-m04-l04 | Typography | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use the Material type scale
- Customise fonts

## Typography
Material 3 defines a type scale: display, headline, title, body and label, each in large/medium/small. Use `Theme.of(context).textTheme.titleLarge` instead of raw font sizes so text stays consistent and respects user scaling. Custom fonts are declared in pubspec.yaml and set with `fontFamily` in ThemeData.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget heading(BuildContext context) => Text(
      'Welcome',
      style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.headlineMedium,
    );
```

## Summary
- Use textTheme styles rather than fixed sizes.
- Use copyWith to tweak a style.

@lesson flutter-m04-l05 | AppBar | 8
## Learning objectives
- Configure titles, actions and scrolling behaviour

## AppBar
`AppBar` shows the screen title, a leading back/menu button and actions. In Material 3 it changes colour slightly when content scrolls under it. Use `SliverAppBar.medium` or `.large` for collapsing titles.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

PreferredSizeWidget bar() => AppBar(
      title: const Text('Courses'),
      actions: [IconButton(onPressed: () {}, icon: const Icon(Icons.search), tooltip: 'Search')],
    );
```

## Summary
- AppBar holds title, leading and actions.
- Always give icon buttons a tooltip for accessibility.

@lesson flutter-m04-l06 | NavigationBar | 8
## Learning objectives
- Switch between top-level destinations

## NavigationBar
`NavigationBar` is the Material 3 bottom navigation for 3 to 5 top-level destinations. Keep the selected index in state and switch the body accordingly.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class Shell extends StatefulWidget {
  const Shell({super.key});
  @override
  State<Shell> createState() => _ShellState();
}

class _ShellState extends State<Shell> {
  int _index = 0;
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Scaffold(
        body: Center(child: Text('Tab $_index')),
        bottomNavigationBar: NavigationBar(
          selectedIndex: _index,
          onDestinationSelected: (i) => setState(() => _index = i),
          destinations: const [
            NavigationDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.home), label: 'Home'),
            NavigationDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.person), label: 'Profile'),
          ],
        ),
      );
}
```

## Summary
- Use NavigationBar for 3–5 destinations.
- Use NavigationRail on wide screens.

@lesson flutter-m04-l07 | NavigationDrawer | 8
## Learning objectives
- Provide many destinations in a side drawer

## NavigationDrawer
`NavigationDrawer` lists destinations in a panel that slides in from the side. Use it when you have more destinations than a bottom bar can hold.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget drawer() => NavigationDrawer(
      selectedIndex: 0,
      children: const [
        Padding(padding: EdgeInsets.all(16), child: Text('Bimo')),
        NavigationDrawerDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.home), label: Text('Home')),
        NavigationDrawerDestination(icon: Icon(Icons.settings), label: Text('Settings')),
      ],
    );
```

## Summary
- Put it in Scaffold.drawer.
- Good for apps with many sections.

@lesson flutter-m04-l08 | Buttons | 8
## Learning objectives
- Choose the right button for emphasis

## Buttons
Material 3 buttons by emphasis: `FilledButton` (highest), `FilledButton.tonal`, `ElevatedButton`, `OutlinedButton`, `TextButton` (lowest). Passing `onPressed: null` disables a button.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget actions() => Row(children: [
      FilledButton(onPressed: () {}, child: const Text('Save')),
      const SizedBox(width: 8),
      OutlinedButton(onPressed: () {}, child: const Text('Cancel')),
    ]);
```

## Summary
- One primary (filled) action per screen.
- onPressed: null disables the button.

@lesson flutter-m04-l09 | Cards | 8
## Learning objectives
- Group related content in cards

## Cards
`Card` is a surface with rounded corners. Material 3 has filled, elevated and outlined variants. Wrap content in `InkWell` inside the card to make it tappable with a ripple.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget lessonCard() => Card.outlined(
      child: InkWell(
        onTap: () {},
        child: const ListTile(title: Text('Variables'), subtitle: Text('8 min')),
      ),
    );
```

## Summary
- Cards group related content.
- Use InkWell for tap feedback.

@lesson flutter-m04-l10 | Dialogs | 8
## Learning objectives
- Show confirmations with showDialog

## Dialogs
Dialogs interrupt the user for important decisions. `showDialog` returns a Future with the value passed to `Navigator.pop`.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Future<bool> confirmDelete(BuildContext context) async {
  final ok = await showDialog<bool>(
    context: context,
    builder: (ctx) => AlertDialog(
      title: const Text('Delete note?'),
      content: const Text('This cannot be undone.'),
      actions: [
        TextButton(onPressed: () => Navigator.pop(ctx, false), child: const Text('Cancel')),
        FilledButton(onPressed: () => Navigator.pop(ctx, true), child: const Text('Delete')),
      ],
    ),
  );
  return ok ?? false;
}
```

## Summary
- Await showDialog for the result.
- Use dialogs sparingly for important choices.

@lesson flutter-m04-l11 | Snackbars | 8
## Learning objectives
- Give brief feedback with SnackBar

## Snackbars
A SnackBar shows a short message at the bottom, optionally with one action such as Undo. Show it with `ScaffoldMessenger.of(context)`.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void saved(BuildContext context) => ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(
      SnackBar(content: const Text('Saved'), action: SnackBarAction(label: 'Undo', onPressed: () {})),
    );
```

## Summary
- SnackBars are brief and non-blocking.
- Offer Undo for destructive actions.

@lesson flutter-m04-l12 | Bottom sheets | 8
## Learning objectives
- Present options with modal bottom sheets

## Bottom sheets
`showModalBottomSheet` slides a panel up from the bottom for contextual options. Use `isScrollControlled: true` for tall content and `showDragHandle: true` for a grabber.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Future<void> sheet(BuildContext context) => showModalBottomSheet<void>(
      context: context,
      showDragHandle: true,
      builder: (_) => const ListTile(leading: Icon(Icons.share), title: Text('Share')),
    );
```

## Summary
- Bottom sheets show contextual actions.
- They return a Future like dialogs.

@lesson flutter-m04-l13 | FloatingActionButton | 8
## Learning objectives
- Highlight the main action of a screen

## FloatingActionButton
A FAB represents the primary action on a screen, such as creating a note. Variants: `FloatingActionButton.small`, `.large` and `.extended` with a label.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget fab() => FloatingActionButton.extended(
      onPressed: () {},
      icon: const Icon(Icons.add),
      label: const Text('New note'),
    );
```

## Summary
- Use at most one FAB per screen.
- Extended FABs add a label for clarity.

@lesson flutter-m04-l14 | Tabs | 8
## Learning objectives
- Organise content into tabs

## Tabs
`DefaultTabController` + `TabBar` + `TabBarView` give swipeable tabs without manual controllers.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget tabs() => const DefaultTabController(
      length: 2,
      child: Scaffold(
        appBar: TabBarWidget(),
        body: TabBarView(children: [Center(child: Text('Lessons')), Center(child: Text('Quizzes'))]),
      ),
    );

class TabBarWidget extends StatelessWidget implements PreferredSizeWidget {
  const TabBarWidget({super.key});
  @override
  Size get preferredSize => const Size.fromHeight(104);
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => AppBar(
        title: const Text('Course'),
        bottom: const TabBar(tabs: [Tab(text: 'Lessons'), Tab(text: 'Quizzes')]),
      );
}
```

## Summary
- DefaultTabController wires TabBar and TabBarView.
- Tabs suit sibling views of similar importance.

@lesson flutter-m04-l15 | Chips | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use chips for filters, choices and tags

## Chips
Chips are compact elements: `FilterChip` for toggling filters, `ChoiceChip` for single choice, `InputChip` for user-entered items and `ActionChip` for actions.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget filters(Set<String> selected, void Function(String) toggle) => Wrap(spacing: 8, children: [
      for (final f in ['Dart', 'Flutter', 'Projects'])
        FilterChip(label: Text(f), selected: selected.contains(f), onSelected: (_) => toggle(f)),
    ]);
```

## Summary
- Pick the chip type by purpose.
- Place many chips in a Wrap.

@lesson flutter-m04-l16 | Menus | 8
## Learning objectives
- Show overflow and dropdown menus

## Menus
`MenuAnchor` and `PopupMenuButton` show lists of actions; `DropdownMenu` lets users pick a value.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget overflow() => PopupMenuButton<String>(
      onSelected: (v) {},
      itemBuilder: (_) => const [
        PopupMenuItem(value: 'rename', child: Text('Rename')),
        PopupMenuItem(value: 'delete', child: Text('Delete')),
      ],
    );
```

## Summary
- Overflow menus hide secondary actions.
- DropdownMenu picks a value from a list.

@lesson flutter-m04-l17 | Text fields | 8
## Learning objectives
- Collect text input with TextField

## Text fields
`TextField` handles keyboard input. Set `keyboardType`, `textInputAction` and `obscureText` for passwords, and read the value through `onChanged` or a controller.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget email() => const TextField(
      keyboardType: TextInputType.emailAddress,
      textInputAction: TextInputAction.next,
      decoration: InputDecoration(labelText: 'Email', prefixIcon: Icon(Icons.email)),
    );
```

## Summary
- Choose the right keyboardType.
- Use obscureText for passwords.

@lesson flutter-m04-l18 | Input decoration | 8
## Learning objectives
- Style fields with labels, hints, icons and errors

## Input decoration
`InputDecoration` adds a label, hint, helper/error text, prefix/suffix icons and borders. Setting `errorText` shows the field in an error state.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

InputDecoration deco(String? error) => InputDecoration(
      labelText: 'Password',
      helperText: 'At least 8 characters',
      errorText: error,
      border: const OutlineInputBorder(),
      suffixIcon: const Icon(Icons.visibility),
    );
```

## Summary
- Labels stay visible, unlike hints.
- errorText shows validation messages.

@lesson flutter-m04-l19 | Form validation | 8
## Learning objectives
- Validate fields with Form and TextFormField

## Form validation
Wrap fields in a `Form` with a `GlobalKey<FormState>`. Each `TextFormField` has a `validator` returning an error string or null; call `formKey.currentState!.validate()` on submit.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

final formKey = GlobalKey<FormState>();

Widget form(VoidCallback onValid) => Form(
      key: formKey,
      child: Column(children: [
        TextFormField(
          decoration: const InputDecoration(labelText: 'Name'),
          validator: (v) => (v == null || v.trim().isEmpty) ? 'Name is required' : null,
        ),
        FilledButton(
          onPressed: () { if (formKey.currentState!.validate()) onValid(); },
          child: const Text('Submit'),
        ),
      ]),
    );
```

## Summary
- validator returns an error message or null.
- validate() checks every field at once.

@lesson flutter-m04-l20 | Light and dark themes | 8
## Learning objectives
- Support light, dark and system themes

## Light and dark themes
Provide `theme` and `darkTheme` to MaterialApp and choose with `themeMode`. Using colour roles means most widgets adapt automatically.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget app(ThemeMode mode) => MaterialApp(
      theme: ThemeData(colorSchemeSeed: Colors.indigo),
      darkTheme: ThemeData(colorSchemeSeed: Colors.indigo, brightness: Brightness.dark),
      themeMode: mode,
      home: const Scaffold(body: Center(child: Text('Hello'))),
    );
```

## Summary
- theme + darkTheme + themeMode.
- Never hard-code white/black text.

@lesson flutter-m04-l21 | Dynamic colors | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use the user's wallpaper colours on Android 12+

## Dynamic colors
On Android 12+, Material You can derive colours from the wallpaper. The `dynamic_color` package provides `DynamicColorBuilder`, which gives you light and dark schemes or null (fall back to your seed scheme).

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

ColorScheme pick(ColorScheme? dynamic) =>
    dynamic ?? ColorScheme.fromSeed(seedColor: const Color(0xFF6750F5));
```

## Summary
- Dynamic colour personalises the app.
- Always provide a fallback scheme.

@lesson flutter-m04-l22 | Accessibility | 8
## Learning objectives
- Make Material UIs usable for everyone

## Accessibility
Use semantic labels (`tooltip`, `semanticLabel`, `Semantics`), touch targets of at least 48x48 dp, sufficient contrast, and support text scaling. Test with TalkBack/VoiceOver.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Widget icon() => const Icon(Icons.delete, semanticLabel: 'Delete lesson');

Widget labelled() => Semantics(
      label: 'Progress 40 percent',
      child: const LinearProgressIndicator(value: 0.4),
    );
```

## Summary
- Label icons and custom widgets.
- Respect 48 dp targets and text scaling.
